    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Two eighteens, three seventeens — and the two eighteens are exactly the two windows that carry three dots on every string."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "None of that has to be memorised dot by dot. The roots came from the caged-fretboard pathway; "
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " came from chapter 2; the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " came from chapter 3. This chapter added two rules, not twenty-six new dots: a "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits one fret above every "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and a "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits one fret below every "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Find a chord tone and both new notes are already located, on every string, in every window. Honestly: a window's fixed edges can cut a pair apart, so the note it locates falls outside the frame — that happens four times in the G form, once in the D form, and nowhere else."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is one fret above every "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". A "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is one fret below every "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". That's the whole scale, once you already know the chord tones."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "caged-ladder",
      "props": {
        "root": "A",
        "quality": "minor"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The ladder marks roots only, whatever the layer — there's no "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " or "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to find on it, and there never will be. That's no longer a limitation. From a root, the two rules above generate everything else, so the minimal map is now enough."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "What each degree is actually for:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "text": "1",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "text": "b3",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " and "
          },
          {
            "text": "5",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " — the chord. Where a phrase arrives."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "4",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " and "
          },
          {
            "text": "b7",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " — never closer than two frets to any chord tone. They move a line without arguing with anything."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "2",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " — a whole step above home and a fret below the "
          },
          {
            "text": "b3",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": ". Consonant over an "
          },
          {
            "text": "Am",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": ". It leans up, and it's what lets a line walk instead of jump."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "b6",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " — a fret above the "
          },
          {
            "text": "5",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": ". The darkest note in the scale, and the one that will fight an "
          },
          {
            "text": "Am",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " if you sit on it. Place it, then move."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 3 found one fret where all six strings sound a note of the scale: fret 5, and only fret 5. With every degree in play there are four such frets — "
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "10",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "12",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and "
        },
        {
          "text": "12",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " again, an octave up. The reason fret 5 stood alone before is now visible: frets "
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "12",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " both carry a "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — the open "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " string, and its octave — and fret "
        },
        {
          "text": "10",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " carries a "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", at "
        },
        {
          "text": "3·10",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Fret 5 is the one fret whose six notes dodge both of the notes this chapter added, which is exactly why it always felt like the safe place to stand."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One seam is short a degree. Three of the four overlaps between neighbouring windows hold all seven degrees. The fourth — frets "
        },
        {
          "text": "4–5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", where the G form meets the E form — holds only six, and the missing one is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ": no "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits at fret 4 or fret 5 on any string. The safest corner of the neck, the one every self-taught player already stands in, turns out to be the one corner the darkest note in the scale can't reach."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's what this chapter has actually been building toward. A natural minor and C major are the same seven notes, so every dot drawn in this chapter is also a dot on the C major neck the caged-fretboard pathway mapped — same frets, same fingers, seven different names. "
        },
        {
          "text": "One Window, Two Names",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-one-window-two-names"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " proved it for a single span; it's true of the whole neck now. And the two semitones are the same two frets either way: "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". In C major, that "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " leaning up into the "
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and that "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " leaning on the "
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". In A minor, the same "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " leaning up into the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and the same "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " leaning on the "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". The leaning notes never moved. What changed is which chord tone is sitting underneath them."
        }
      ]
    },
